      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "# Lab: Cost Function for Climate Impact on Crop Yield Prediction\n\n## The Global Challenge\n\nClimate change is one of the most pressing issues facing humanity. Among its many consequences, the impact on agricultural yields threatens food security for billions of people. Farmers, governments, and international organizations urgently need tools to understand how rising temperatures affect crop productivity so they can plan adaptation strategies, allocate resources, and prevent famine.\n\nThis lab demonstrates how the **cost function** in linear regression can help build predictive models that quantify the relationship between temperature increases and crop yield changes. While simplified, this mirrors real research conducted by organizations like the FAO (Food and Agriculture Organization), IPCC, and agricultural research institutes worldwide.\n\n## Why This Matters\n\nConsider these real-world stakes:\n- A 1 degree Celsius increase in average growing-season temperature can reduce wheat yields by approximately 5-10% in tropical regions.\n- Over 800 million people currently face chronic food insecurity.\n- By 2050, global food production must increase by 60% to feed 9.7 billion people, while climate change simultaneously threatens to reduce yields.\n- Inaccurate predictions lead to either over-preparation (wasted resources) or under-preparation (humanitarian crises).\n\nThe cost function is the mathematical mechanism that allows us to evaluate and improve our predictions. A model with a high cost makes unreliable predictions, which in this context could mean the difference between adequate preparation and catastrophic food shortages.\n\n## Goals\nIn this lab you will:\n- Implement the cost function J(w,b) for linear regression\n- Understand how cost measures prediction quality in a climate impact model\n- Explore how parameter choices affect prediction accuracy for agricultural planning\n- Visualize cost surfaces to understand optimization landscapes\n- Connect mathematical concepts to real humanitarian outcomes\n\n## Notation Summary\n\n| Notation | Description | Python Variable |\n|:---------|:------------|:----------------|\n| $m$ | Number of regional observations | `m` |\n| $x^{(i)}$ | Temperature anomaly for region $i$ (degrees C above baseline) | `x_train[i]` |\n| $y^{(i)}$ | Crop yield change for region $i$ (% change from baseline) | `y_train[i]` |\n| $w$ | Yield sensitivity to temperature (slope) | `w` |\n| $b$ | Baseline yield change at zero anomaly (intercept) | `b` |\n| $f_{w,b}(x^{(i)})$ | Predicted yield change for temperature anomaly $x^{(i)}$ | `f_wb` |\n| $J(w,b)$ | Cost function measuring total prediction error | `total_cost` |"
    },
